1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,277
An open hand catches a ball.

2
00:00:02,277 --> 00:00:04,521
An empty seat takes a new passenger.

3
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:06,448
A free hook holds a new coat.

4
00:00:06,848 --> 00:00:08,863
Something open takes something new.

5
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:11,342
An alkene's double bond
works the same way.

6
00:00:11,792 --> 00:00:14,451
At his study desk, Aarav's notes
list eight reactions of alkenes.

7
00:00:14,451 --> 00:00:15,518
They all look different.

8
00:00:15,868 --> 00:00:17,247
Then he spots it.

9
00:00:17,247 --> 00:00:22,022
Almost every one is one move: open
the pi bond, add two new pieces.

10
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:26,454
By the end, you will predict where each
new piece goes, using Markovnikov's

11
00:00:26,454 --> 00:00:27,354
rule.

12
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:29,407
So why are alkenes so ready to react?

13
00:00:29,757 --> 00:00:33,563
The pi electrons sit above and below
the double bond, out in the open.

14
00:00:33,563 --> 00:00:35,804
They attract anything
hungry for electrons.

15
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:37,460
These are called electrophiles.

16
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:41,907
Hydrogen adds over finely divided
nickel, palladium or platinum.

17
00:00:41,907 --> 00:00:43,910
The alkene becomes an alkane.

18
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:45,494
Bromine adds too.

19
00:00:45,494 --> 00:00:47,806
Its reddish orange colour disappears.

20
00:00:47,806 --> 00:00:50,195
That is the test for a double bond.

21
00:00:50,595 --> 00:00:52,540
Hydrogen halides add too.

22
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:57,614
Hydrogen iodide fastest, then hydrogen
bromide, then hydrogen chloride.

23
00:00:58,014 --> 00:01:02,206
In ethene, both carbons are the
same, so it does not matter.

24
00:01:02,206 --> 00:01:03,905
But propene is lopsided.

25
00:01:04,555 --> 00:01:08,858
Let's watch hydrogen bromide meet
propene on our molecule bench.

26
00:01:09,208 --> 00:01:10,108
Step one.

27
00:01:09,873 --> 00:01:12,500
The pi electrons reach
out and grab the hydrogen.

28
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,784
The bromine leaves as bromide,
taking the bond's two electrons.

29
00:01:16,184 --> 00:01:19,157
Now one carbon has lost its
share of the pi electrons.

30
00:01:19,157 --> 00:01:20,334
It becomes positive.

31
00:01:20,334 --> 00:01:21,644
This is a carbocation.

32
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:26,782
If the hydrogen joins the end carbon,
the plus charge sits on the middle

33
00:01:26,782 --> 00:01:27,682
carbon.

34
00:01:27,464 --> 00:01:29,683
That is a secondary carbocation.

35
00:01:30,083 --> 00:01:34,992
If the hydrogen joins the middle
carbon, the plus charge sits on the end.

36
00:01:34,992 --> 00:01:37,071
That is a primary carbocation.

37
00:01:37,471 --> 00:01:39,901
The secondary carbocation is more stable.

38
00:01:39,901 --> 00:01:43,949
Two carbon groups next to it push
in electrons and share the burden.

39
00:01:44,349 --> 00:01:45,249
Step two.

40
00:01:45,036 --> 00:01:47,272
The bromide ion attacks the plus charge.

41
00:01:47,272 --> 00:01:49,374
The major product is two bromopropane.

42
00:01:50,024 --> 00:01:51,928
Markovnikov turned this into a rule.

43
00:01:51,928 --> 00:01:55,348
The negative part of the adding
molecule goes to the carbon with fewer

44
00:01:55,348 --> 00:01:56,248
hydrogens.

45
00:01:56,388 --> 00:01:58,553
Think of it as the rich get richer.

46
00:01:58,553 --> 00:02:02,000
The hydrogen joins the carbon
that already has more hydrogens.

47
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:03,300
Try one.

48
00:02:03,146 --> 00:02:05,173
Propene meets hydrogen chloride.

49
00:02:05,573 --> 00:02:06,473
Pause and think.

50
00:02:06,448 --> 00:02:07,943
Which carbon gets the chlorine?

51
00:02:11,143 --> 00:02:12,043
The middle carbon!

52
00:02:12,038 --> 00:02:13,143
It has fewer hydrogens.

53
00:02:13,143 --> 00:02:14,624
The product is two chloropropane.

54
00:02:15,274 --> 00:02:19,568
But add a peroxide, and hydrogen
bromide adds the other way round.

55
00:02:19,568 --> 00:02:22,692
This is the peroxide
effect, or Kharash effect.

56
00:02:22,692 --> 00:02:23,754
Link by link.

57
00:02:24,104 --> 00:02:25,004
Link one.

58
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:27,346
The peroxide splits into free radicals.

59
00:02:27,346 --> 00:02:31,635
A radical pulls the hydrogen off
hydrogen bromide, and a bromine radical

60
00:02:31,635 --> 00:02:32,623
is set free.

61
00:02:33,373 --> 00:02:34,273
Link two.

62
00:02:34,176 --> 00:02:36,996
The bromine radical
adds to the end carbon.

63
00:02:36,996 --> 00:02:41,169
That leaves the more stable secondary
radical on the middle carbon.

64
00:02:41,919 --> 00:02:42,819
Link three.

65
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:45,986
This radical takes a hydrogen
from another hydrogen bromide.

66
00:02:45,986 --> 00:02:48,957
We get one bromopropane,
and a new bromine radical.

67
00:02:49,707 --> 00:02:50,607
Link four.

68
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:52,009
Why not hydrogen chloride?

69
00:02:52,009 --> 00:02:54,639
Its bond is too strong
for radicals to break.

70
00:02:55,389 --> 00:02:56,289
Link five.

71
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:00,676
The hydrogen iodide bond breaks easily,
but iodine radicals just pair up

72
00:03:00,676 --> 00:03:02,765
into iodine, and do not add.

73
00:03:03,515 --> 00:03:04,530
Remember this.

74
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:06,569
Normally, the rich get richer.

75
00:03:06,569 --> 00:03:09,833
Only hydrogen bromide with
a peroxide flips the rule.

76
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:12,185
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:12,185 --> 00:03:15,212
An alkene gives ethanal
and methanal on ozonolysis.

78
00:03:15,212 --> 00:03:16,541
Which alkene is it?

79
00:03:16,841 --> 00:03:17,741
Step one.

80
00:03:17,578 --> 00:03:21,502
In ozonolysis, ozone adds to the
double bond and makes an ozonide.

81
00:03:21,502 --> 00:03:23,352
Zinc and water then split it.

82
00:03:23,352 --> 00:03:26,782
Each carbon of the double bond
becomes a C double bond O.

83
00:03:27,532 --> 00:03:28,432
Step two.

84
00:03:28,396 --> 00:03:32,955
Write the two pieces with their C
double bond O facing each other.

85
00:03:32,955 --> 00:03:35,545
CH three CHO, and HCHO.

86
00:03:36,295 --> 00:03:37,195
Step three.

87
00:03:37,074 --> 00:03:40,728
Remove both oxygens, and join the
two carbons with a double bond.

88
00:03:41,478 --> 00:03:42,378
Step four.

89
00:03:42,375 --> 00:03:45,952
We get CH three, CH, double bond, CH two.

90
00:03:45,952 --> 00:03:47,591
The alkene is propene.

91
00:03:48,341 --> 00:03:51,442
This is how chemists find
where a double bond sits.

92
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:56,042
Cold concentrated sulphuric acid also
adds by Markovnikov's rule, giving

93
00:03:56,042 --> 00:03:57,732
an alkyl hydrogen sulphate.

94
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:02,685
Water, with a few drops of concentrated
sulphuric acid, adds the same way,

95
00:04:02,685 --> 00:04:03,938
giving an alcohol.

96
00:04:04,338 --> 00:04:09,235
Cold, dilute potassium permanganate,
Baeyer's reagent, adds two OH groups,

97
00:04:09,235 --> 00:04:10,487
giving a glycol.

98
00:04:10,487 --> 00:04:14,457
Its pink colour disappears,
another test for a double bond.

99
00:04:14,857 --> 00:04:18,057
Acidified permanganate goes
further, and cuts the double bond.

100
00:04:18,057 --> 00:04:19,829
But two ene gives ethanoic acid.

101
00:04:20,229 --> 00:04:21,244
And polymerisation.

102
00:04:21,244 --> 00:04:24,712
At high temperature and pressure,
with a catalyst, many ethene molecules

103
00:04:24,712 --> 00:04:25,812
join into polythene.

104
00:04:26,462 --> 00:04:27,923
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:04:28,273 --> 00:04:31,372
Students put the hydrogen on the carbon
with fewer hydrogens, and get one

106
00:04:31,372 --> 00:04:32,542
bromopropane from propene.

107
00:04:32,942 --> 00:04:33,842
Wrong.

108
00:04:33,471 --> 00:04:37,292
Without peroxide, hydrogen goes to
the carbon with more hydrogens, and

109
00:04:37,292 --> 00:04:38,670
bromine to the middle.

110
00:04:38,670 --> 00:04:40,845
Two bromopropane is the major product.

111
00:04:41,495 --> 00:04:43,013
Where is this asked?

112
00:04:43,013 --> 00:04:46,886
Boards ask the rule and the
peroxide effect with mechanism.

113
00:04:46,886 --> 00:04:49,658
Entrance tests ask for the major product.

114
00:04:50,258 --> 00:04:51,474
Exam level now.

115
00:04:51,474 --> 00:04:56,082
Two methyl propene reacts with hydrogen
bromide in the presence of benzoyl

116
00:04:56,082 --> 00:04:56,982
peroxide.

117
00:04:56,866 --> 00:04:58,773
What is the major product?

118
00:04:58,773 --> 00:05:00,041
Pause and solve.

119
00:05:05,441 --> 00:05:06,883
The answer is B.

120
00:05:06,883 --> 00:05:09,120
One bromo two methyl propane.

121
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:14,846
Peroxide plus hydrogen bromide means the
free radical path, against Markovnikov.

122
00:05:15,346 --> 00:05:19,850
The bromine radical adds to the end
carbon, leaving a tertiary radical,

123
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:21,385
the most stable kind.

124
00:05:21,885 --> 00:05:23,998
Then the radical takes a hydrogen.

125
00:05:23,998 --> 00:05:26,852
A is the Markovnikov
product, without peroxide.

126
00:05:26,852 --> 00:05:28,745
C and D are not formed here.

127
00:05:29,345 --> 00:05:30,886
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:30,886 --> 00:05:32,470
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:35,670 --> 00:05:37,856
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:38,256 --> 00:05:39,156
One.

131
00:05:38,784 --> 00:05:42,939
Why is a secondary carbocation
more stable than a primary one?

132
00:05:43,739 --> 00:05:44,639
Two.

133
00:05:44,205 --> 00:05:47,488
Why does hydrogen chloride
not show the peroxide effect?

134
00:05:48,288 --> 00:05:49,188
Three.

135
00:05:48,725 --> 00:05:51,151
What does Baeyer's reagent
tell you about a compound?

136
00:05:52,051 --> 00:05:56,501
Next time: why can ethyne give away
a hydrogen like an acid, when ethene

137
00:05:56,501 --> 00:05:57,401
cannot?

138
00:05:57,128 --> 00:05:58,741
We find out in alkynes.
